        reading: [{ heading: "The question that got him killed", body: "Socrates walked around Athens asking people: 'What do you really know?' He didn't write books. He asked questions. He asked politicians what justice was — they couldn't answer. He asked poets what their poems meant — they didn't know. He asked generals what courage was — they fumbled. He wasn't trying to embarrass them. He was trying to show that most people live on beliefs they've never tested. The oracle at Delphi said he was the wisest man in Athens. He said that was because he was the only one who knew he didn't know. For asking too many questions, they sentenced him to death. He drank the poison and died. His last words were about owing a debt to a god. The question that got him killed: have you examined what you believe, or are you just repeating what you heard?" }],
