        reading: [{ heading: "The examined and the borrowed", body: "Socrates' claim in the Apology: 'The unexamined life is not worth living.' He said this at his trial, where he was sentenced to death for corrupting youth and impiety. The claim is not that unexamined lives are bad. It's that they're not fully yours. If your beliefs are inherited, untested, repeated from authority — you're living someone else's life in your body. The examined life is the one where you've asked: why do I believe this? What would change my mind? What if I'm wrong? Socrates didn't claim to have answers. He claimed to have the method: ask, answer, find the contradiction, revise. The oracle said he was wisest because he alone knew he didn't know. The question for you: what do you 'know' that you've never tested?" }],
