        objective: "The student can name one virtue (courage, generosity, honesty) and identify what 'too much' and 'too little' look like.",
        parentBriefing: "Aristotle's golden mean: virtue is the right amount between two extremes. Courage is between cowardice and recklessness. Generosity is between stinginess and waste. The key: 'right amount' is not 'medium amount' — it depends on the situation. Sometimes the right amount of courage is a lot. Keep it concrete with virtues they know.",
        hardEdges: ["Don't say 'be moderate in everything.' Sometimes the right amount is extreme — a lot of courage in a fire, a lot of honesty when someone is in danger.", "The mean is not mathematical. It's contextual. That's the hard part — and that's why it can't be automated."],
        reading: [{ heading: "The seasoning theory", body: "Aristotle said being good is like seasoning food. Too little salt: bland. Too much salt: inedible. Just right: delicious. That's the golden mean. Courage: too little is cowardice, too much is recklessness, just right is courage. Generosity: too little is stinginess, too much is waste, just right is generosity. Honesty: too little is lying, too much is cruelty (telling someone they're ugly and calling it honesty), just right is truthfulness with care. The tricky part: 'just right' changes. The right amount of courage in a fire is a LOT. The right amount of courage at a party is a little. You can't measure it with a ruler. You have to feel it — and practice. That's why Aristotle said virtue is a habit, not a rule." }],
        bigIdea: "Virtue is the right amount — and the right amount changes depending on the situation. That's why it takes practice, not rules.",
