        objective: "The student can state Aristotle's golden mean, explain why it cannot be reduced to a formula, and evaluate whether AI can compute virtuous action.",
        parentBriefing: "This is the sitting where Aristotle's ethics meets the limits of computation. The mean is contextual: right amount, right time, right person, right reason. That's four variables that require judgment, not calculation. The AI connection: a model can give you a rule. It can't assess the situation. Only the person in the situation can find the mean.",
        hardEdges: ["Don't reduce the mean to 'everything in moderation.' That's the cliché. Aristotle's version is harder: right amount, right time, right person, right reason.", "If they say 'AI can just be programmed with context-aware rules' — push back. The mean requires understanding the situation, not just processing data about it. That's the Searle problem in ethical clothing."],
        reading: [{ heading: "The mean that can't be computed", body: "Aristotle's golden mean: virtue is the right amount, at the right time, toward the right person, for the right reason. Courage is not a fixed quantity. It's the right amount of fear and action in this situation, with this person, for this reason. Generosity is not 'give 10%.' It's the right amount of resources to the right person at the right time. The mean is not mathematical — it's contextual. Aristotle said this is why virtue cannot be taught as a rule. You develop the capacity to find the mean by practicing it, not by memorizing it. The AI connection: a model can give you a rule ('be honest'). It cannot assess whether honesty is the right virtue in this situation, at this time, toward this person, for this reason. That assessment requires judgment — the capacity to read a situation and respond appropriately. Aristotle said this capacity is what makes ethics human. A world where AI computes the mean is a world where ethics has been reduced to calculation. Aristotle would say: that's not ethics. That's a formula." }],
        bigIdea: "The mean is not a formula. It's a practiced judgment in a specific situation. That's why it can't be automated — and why ethics is irreducibly human.",
