        reading: [{ heading: "The tiny blocks", body: "Democritus said: everything is made of tiny blocks called atoms, moving through empty space called void. Atoms are too small to see. But we know they're there because the reasoning works — if you combine them different ways, you get different things. Wood, water, air, you. Everything. 'By convention sweet, by convention bitter; in reality, atoms and void.' That means: sweetness isn't in the sugar. It's in how the atoms hit your tongue. The sugar is atoms. The tongue is atoms. The sweetness is what happens when they meet. Is a person just atoms? Democritus said yes. But 'just' is doing a lot of work. A song is just notes, but the song is real. A person is just atoms, but the person is real. The question: does 'just' mean 'only' — or does the arrangement matter?" }],
        bigIdea: "Everything is atoms — but 'just atoms' doesn't mean 'just nothing.' The arrangement is what makes a person real.",
        tryThis: [{ title: "Build with atoms", minutes: 12, steps: ["Name three things made of the same atoms in different arrangements (carbon: diamond, graphite, you).", "If a person is atoms arranged a certain way, what makes the arrangement special?", "Talk About It: is a song 'just notes'? Is a person 'just atoms'? What does 'just' mean?"] }],
